today we're going to be speaking with  sheila alechi who is a reporter with  icij and also coordinates the  organization's partners in asia and  western europe  thanks so much for talking to us today  thank you  so  my audience has certainly heard of the  pandora papers we've reported  i don't know about extensively but we've  reported about the pandora papers for  people who maybe aren't familiar with  what these documents are how do you  generally summarize what the documents  are  well i would say that pandora papers is  an investigation based on a leak of  about 12 million documents  confidential financial records coming  from 14 different  financial service providers from around  the world from the most secrecy  secretive jurisdictions including  seychelles cyprus bvi and so forth and  what these documents tell us  is how the offshore financial system  works but not just that also how the the  global economy in a way works because  the system that thrives on secrecy  and  anonymity is basically a big part of the  global economy right now  and  some of the biggest headlines related to  individual prominent individuals  politicians elected officials  functionaries of other kinds well-known  people  um  i i don't know if secretly is the right  word but certainly behind the scenes  using a lot of these other other  mechanisms and instruments for their own  personal finances is that a fair  assessment  i think so what we found was that uh  more than 300 politicians including more  than 30 current and former uh country  leaders used shell companies and  offshore trusts to hide wealth  from their own citizens as well so  public  elected officials  such as the president of kenya the king  of jordan  the then prime minister czech republic  and other prominent politicians um used  this kind of structures to  hide wealth or buy  real estate around the world and um  obviously we have to be careful here we  don't want to say that all of this is  illegal  much of this actually is a legal and  legitimate but the problem here is  actually that  it allows um  people to avoid scrutiny um and so it  can be used um  by um you know husbands who don't want  their divorces to know about their  assets um as well as criminals who don't  want authorities to know about their  assets  how does the icij um obtain these  documents to the extent that you can  talk to us about the process  yes we don't comment on our sources  we obtained these documents and  what we did initially was  fact checking most of them and trying to  figure out whether they were actually  whether they were accurate and where  they were  you know fake fake documents and  the way we do this is a process that we  have learned to do over time because  this is not the first offshore financial  project that we do  we obviously check the leaked documents  against public records  we always  involve journalists from many different  countries who have the expertise and the  sources who allow us also to verify the  information whether it's true or not and  as you know we don't publish we don't  publish the whole data set because there  are confidential information and also um  you know information that is may not be  true so um we try to um you know go  publish publish only stories that we are  able to verify and um and um also ask  comment for yeah i wanted that was going  to be my next question about asking for  comment in many types of stories  uh when someone is written about you ask  that person for a comment there's a  deadline they have the opportunity to  say something or to say no comment or to  not even respond at all  here  the the story is really the documents  and if the documents are accurate  the concept of a comment is a little bit  different right because it's basically  do you what exactly are we asking for  when we ask for comment about a document  well we want to know the context because  sometimes you don't have the story in  one document you may know that one  person set up a company and that's it  you may know what it is for sometimes  you don't even know what the purpose of  this company is so obviously we want to  know we also want to know the purpose um  for example if it was for tax reasons or  not um and then obviously you know we  also want to um just give the  opportunity to anybody um to  to say to have their say and um seij  alone we are about 15 reporters and  editors on staff and we sent more than  400 comment requests just for this  investigation excluding all the requests  that our partners  have sent so every name that is  on our story  we will will be  you know fact checked and  they will have the opportunity to  comment now out of the 400 how many  chose to comment well i don't know the  exact percentage but i must say that  what i've noticed  is that over time people especially  politicians have developed a very good  strategy and most of them did not  respond or did not respond adequately or  they chose not to respond to our  questions and wait for after publication  to go on their social media and facebook  and you know give their own opinion  there directly to their  followers  you mentioned that a lot of what is  revealed is not against the law  but it can be used in ways to  circumvent reporting requirements or  there's a lot of different ways in which  these mechanisms can be used how much of  of the investigation is  talking to lawyers about  is there anything illegal here and  highlighting the illegal  a lot a lot of because we are  journalists we are not experts obviously  we've been doing this for many years so  there are some basic um  you know concepts and knowledge that we  have but at the same time you also want  to be sure that you know  you are up to date with the  most recent legislation or regulation  etc and also here we talk about  hundreds of countries so each country  has its specific regulation and after  panama papers which is an investigation  we did in 2016 um some laws changed so  you also want to be sure that what  you're reporting now is um obviously up  to date um so  yes we we love experts um  and  they are they they always help us  understand um  the context  sometimes when we have teams reporting  teams from many different countries we  also have experts from many different  countries who kind of um tell us what's  going on  so for this i want to give a little  context for my next question in the  united states certainly and i think this  happens in other countries too whenever  there's a leak of information  if you like the content of the leak you  focus on the content and you ignore how  the documents were obtained if you don't  like the content of the leak you ignore  the content and you say how dare they  get these private documents you focus  only on how it was obtained  when we're talking about banking records  i mean there certainly are  privacy laws and different countries  have different rules the same as with  medical records with with banking  information  how does the icij  think about  that at the end of the day these  documents are  presumably documents that are are  believed to be private documents and how  what is the sort of ethical approach to  that  so first of all whenever we publish a  name or a story we always ask whether  it's in the public interest so i'm not  going to publish my neighbors you know  banking records nobody cares about that  but i do care about a politician i do  care also about a powerful business  person who may have a political ties or  any impact on a community um  obviously i do care about criminals so  um that's some ethical um discussion  that we you know we have always have  before uh publishing anything um and  then again you go and ask for comments  so you don't do it behind the the back  you know like you always um try to  confront the the people that you're  writing about um so it's not a surprise  um and  when i say we go to co for comment we  have um  you know we we ask about at least five  weeks before publication so that we  can always ask at least three times  um calling i have sent for example last  time i sent  an email uh not an email a registered  mail to myanmar you know because  there was only a physical address that i  could use and you try whatever you can  are there um are is this the type of  investigation where for your team  there are physical security concerns for  the reporters that are doing the  investigating  so lucky  icij's team most of it is based in the  u.s or in europe  but of course we work with journalists  from around the world and uh you know  from russia  hong kong for example arab countries and  we have a lot of and also in latin  america many latin american countries  have freedom issues so um  more than the ics staff obviously we're  always concerned for our partners and we  work with them every day we're always in  touch so if there is an issue for them  we try to to help them for example one  thing that we do is if a partner cannot  publish a story in their own country  there may be a partner in a neighboring  country that can publish that story for  them so that the person or the reporters  in trouble can report on something that  has already been reported without  exposing themselves so we try to so  that's why collaboration is great  because it also help protect each other  um and especially people in you know  more vulnerable vulnerable situations  whenever there are these these big leaks  and revelations  um there's the question of okay so what  is the ideal  consequence or outcome  with you know you look at the edward  snowden nsa leaks and there are a lot of  people who said well the the outcome  should be now that people have been made  aware of these techniques  those should be changed they should be  made illegal or you know there's kind of  like a clear path what's the goal here  is it sort of international  tax and finance policy reform or what  would be the outcome that would be an  ideal outcome  well surely transparency is something  that we will hope  to have more around the world you know  and after panama papers for example some  countries introduced laws to  for example reports record the  beneficial ownership  and so forth and every time obviously  that we do a project like this new  loopholes emerge for example this time  something interesting had to do with the  us and what we learned was that while  smaller jurisdictions for example like  bahamas were trying to improve their own  system uh huge and you know economies  like the us that were actually becoming  um the go-to place for people to hide  money um and so this time we really hope  that if the us that actually step up and  improve  their own regulations  when it comes to people like elton john  jackie chan you know shakira named in  the papers  talk to me about the public interest  there and to be clear i'm not saying  there is no public interest it seems  different than an elected official that  might influence policy however so i'm  curious about the public interest when  it comes to entertainers you know um  i understand that there is a difference  between a politician an elected official  and a pop star but at the same time  these people have a lot of influence  even if it's just uh um  you know on people a lot of impact on on  every everybody um and so they kind of  like they they chose a public life in a  way and so obviously um  the their their finances um if obviously  if we find something that is worth  reporting um then that can be um you  know um put on scrutiny um at the same  time we always go and ask for comment  and so for example um there were a few  of these um celebrities that  came back saying that they had done  nothing wrong and um which we also  reported like models etc um  but you know this is a system that  rewarding  that we want people to understand why it  is significant at the same time whether  if while it is legitimate um in some  cases you know you still have tax  revenues that are lost  so we want the public to be informed and  whether it's a politician or a celebrity  that contributes to the system i think  it's still worth talking about it  well it's it's an incredible  investigation and i encourage uh people  in in my audience to  go to the icij website and there's so so  much documentation there we've been  speaking with sheila alechi who is a  reporter with the icij  i really appreciate your time today  thanks so much for talking to us  thank you

